  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 AM EDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nSkies remain mostly clear across Upper Michigan early this morning \nunder the continued influence of high pressure extending westward \ninto the Great Lakes from Quebec. This has allowed for another night \nof efficient radiational cooling, although temps are running a touch \nhigher than the last few nights owing to light southerly low level \nflow. This should keep any frost confined to the coldest interior \nspots through daybreak. Also seeing a few reports of fog over \nnorthwest WI early this morning, and do expect at least some patchy \nfog development over parts of the UP this morning as dewpoint \ndepressions continue to narrow. Finally, extensive high level cloud \ncover is noted upstream over Minnesota, and expect that to \neventually spread over our area today in conjunction with a weak \nshortwave trough crossing the region. Recent model runs have \ncontinued to back off of the already minimal precipitation chances \nwith this feature, now keeping the forecast completely dry across \nthe west today as HREF has chances for any measurable rain in the \nsingle digits. Temperatures will mostly be in the mid to upper 60s \nagain this afternoon, with dewpoints mixing out nicely. This will \nallow RH values to drop into the low to mid 40s in interior \nlocations, especially across the eastern UP where approaching cloud \ncover will hold off the longest. Do not anticipate any significant \nfire weather concerns given winds staying below 15 mph.\n\nUpper level ridging rebuilds over the region Friday into this \nweekend, yielding continued benign weather with highs climbing into \nthe upper 60s to low 70s into the early part of next week. Another \nweak disturbance could graze the western UP late this weekend, but \nguidance is trending in a similar direction to today's shortwave in \nterms of precipitation, capping PoPs at a slight chance with the NBM \ndepicting only around a 25% chance of measurable rainfall over \nroughly the western third of the UP late Saturday into Sunday. The \nforecast therefore remains dry across the bulk of the area well into \nnext week. Ensembles continue to show good agreement on the ridge \nfinally breaking down over eastern Canada by the middle of next \nweek. This will open the door for a trough to swing through the \nGreat Lakes and bring the next chance for widespread rainfall, \nalthough details are still of course very much up in the air at this \npoint in the forecast.",
